<div class="quiz"><div class="quiz__score-tracker" style="display:none;"></div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q1b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">1</span> <div><p><strong>In Meditation I, Descartes does not try to examine every one of his beliefs individually. What is his actual strategy for doubting them?</strong></p></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1a" id="q1a">
        <label for="q1a">He consults other philosophers to identify which beliefs are unreliable.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1b" id="q1b">
        <label for="q1b">He targets the underlying principles or sources on which groups of beliefs rest, so that doubting the source brings down everything built on it.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1c" id="q1c">
        <label for="q1c">He uses scientific experiments to test whether his sensory beliefs are accurate.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1d" id="q1d">
        <label for="q1d">He identifies the beliefs most likely to be false and discards only those.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Rather than examining each belief one by one, Descartes attacks the foundations — primarily sensory experience — so that anything built on those foundations collapses with them.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Descartes explicitly says he cannot examine every belief individually. Instead he targets the underlying sources or principles — if the foundation is unreliable, everything built on it is too.</div>
    <button class="quiz__clear" type="button" style="display:none;">Clear</button>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q2b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">2</span> <div><p><strong>Why does Descartes need the Dream Argument if he has already shown that the senses sometimes deceive him?</strong></p></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2a" id="q2a">
        <label for="q2a">The Dream Argument applies specifically to mathematical beliefs, which the senses-deceive argument does not reach.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2b" id="q2b">
        <label for="q2b">The senses-deceive argument only covers distant or unclear perceptions; the Dream Argument extends doubt to vivid, immediate experience — even sitting by a fire.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2c" id="q2c">
        <label for="q2c">The Dream Argument proves that the senses are always unreliable, whereas the earlier argument only showed they are sometimes unreliable.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2d" id="q2d">
        <label for="q2d">The Dream Argument is needed to doubt moral and ethical beliefs, which the senses-deceive argument cannot touch.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. The first argument only undermines beliefs about things perceived unclearly or at a distance. The Dream Argument extends doubt to immediate, vivid perceptions — even the seemingly obvious fact that Descartes is sitting by a fire holding paper.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Descartes notes that the first argument only applies to distant or unclear perceptions. The Dream Argument is needed to cast doubt on immediate, vivid experience — which the first argument cannot reach.</div>
    <button class="quiz__clear" type="button" style="display:none;">Clear</button>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q3b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">3</span> <div><p><strong>What is the primary philosophical purpose of the Evil Demon hypothesis in Meditation I?</strong></p></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3a" id="q3a">
        <label for="q3a">To argue that God might be evil and therefore an unreliable guarantor of knowledge.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3b" id="q3b">
        <label for="q3b">To extend doubt to mathematical and logical truths, which the Dream Argument left untouched.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3c" id="q3c">
        <label for="q3c">To demonstrate that the external world definitely does not exist.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3d" id="q3d">
        <label for="q3d">To argue that human cognitive faculties are inherently unreliable in all circumstances.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. The Dream Argument left mathematical truths seemingly intact (2+2=4 whether awake or dreaming). The Evil Demon closes this gap by positing a deceiver powerful enough to corrupt even seemingly self-evident rational truths.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Descartes notes that even during dreaming, mathematical truths seem secure. The Evil Demon is introduced specifically to cast doubt on those — a being powerful enough to make even clear rational intuitions unreliable.</div>
    <button class="quiz__clear" type="button" style="display:none;">Clear</button>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q4c">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">4</span> <div><strong>Why is the cogito — "I think, therefore I am" — said to survive even the Evil Demon hypothesis?</strong></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4a" id="q4a">
        <label for="q4a">Because God would not allow the demon to deceive Descartes about his own existence.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4b" id="q4b">
        <label for="q4b">Because sensory experience is not involved — the cogito is a purely rational truth.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4c" id="q4c">
        <label for="q4c">Because deception itself requires a subject being deceived — the very act of doubting confirms that something exists to do the doubting.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4d" id="q4d">
        <label for="q4d">Because Descartes has already proven that the demon cannot affect mathematical truths.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Even if an evil demon is deceiving Descartes about everything, there must be something being deceived. The act of doubting is itself a form of thinking, and thinking requires a thinker. The cogito is self-confirming in a way no other belief is.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">The cogito survives the Evil Demon because deception requires a subject. If the demon is deceiving Descartes, then Descartes must exist to be deceived. Doubting is a form of thinking, and thinking confirms existence.</div>
    <button class="quiz__clear" type="button" style="display:none;">Clear</button>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q5b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">5</span> <div><strong>What is Descartes trying to show with the wax argument in Meditation II?</strong></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5a" id="q5a">
        <label for="q5a">That physical objects like wax are not real, because their properties change constantly.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5b" id="q5b">
        <label for="q5b">That our genuine understanding of physical things comes through the intellect, not through the senses — and therefore the mind is better known than material objects.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5c" id="q5c">
        <label for="q5c">That the senses can be trusted when they report on ordinary everyday objects like wax.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5d" id="q5d">
        <label for="q5d">That God created the physical world, as evidenced by the orderly properties of material objects.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. When wax melts, all its sensory properties change — yet we still know it is the same wax. This shows that what we understand by &quot;wax&quot; is grasped by the intellect, not the senses. The mind is therefore more reliably known than any physical object.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">The wax argument shows that genuine knowledge of physical things comes through the intellect, not the senses. Since all the wax's sensory properties change when melted, the &quot;wax&quot; we understand must be grasped by the mind alone — which shows the mind is better known than the body.</div>
    <button class="quiz__clear" type="button" style="display:none;">Clear</button>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q6c">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">6</span> <div><strong>After establishing that he exists, Descartes asks what kind of thing he is. What is his answer at the end of Meditation II?</strong></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6a" id="q6a">
        <label for="q6a">A bodily creature whose existence depends on the physical world.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6b" id="q6b">
        <label for="q6b">A soul temporarily inhabiting a body, capable of surviving death.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6c" id="q6c">
        <label for="q6c">A thinking thing (res cogitans) — something that doubts, understands, affirms, denies, imagines, and perceives.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6d" id="q6d">
        <label for="q6d">A rational animal, defined by both reason and physical embodiment.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Descartes concludes he is a &quot;res cogitans&quot; — a thinking thing. Since the body's existence is still in doubt, he can only define himself through mental activities: doubting, understanding, affirming, denying, imagining, perceiving.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Since the existence of the body is still in doubt at the end of Meditation II, Descartes can only say he is a &quot;thinking thing&quot; (res cogitans) — defined entirely by mental activity, not physical embodiment.</div>
    <button class="quiz__clear" type="button" style="display:none;">Clear</button>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q7b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">7</span> <div><strong>In Meditation III, Descartes argues that his idea of God proves God's existence. What causal principle does this argument depend on?</strong></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7a" id="q7a">
        <label for="q7a">Every idea in the mind must have been experienced through the senses at some earlier time.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7b" id="q7b">
        <label for="q7b">A cause must contain at least as much reality as its effect — you cannot get more out of a cause than was put in.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7c" id="q7c">
        <label for="q7c">God, being omnipotent, can produce any effect regardless of his own nature.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7d" id="q7d">
        <label for="q7d">Ideas of infinite things are always more reliable than ideas of finite things.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Descartes applies the principle that a cause must have at least as much reality as its effect. Since the idea of God has infinite &quot;objective reality&quot; (infinite perfection as its content), its cause must also be infinitely perfect — only God could produce such an idea.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">The causal argument relies on the principle that a cause must contain at least as much reality as its effect. The idea of God has infinite perfection as its content, so only something with infinite perfection — God — could be its cause.</div>
    <button class="quiz__clear" type="button" style="display:none;">Clear</button>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q8c">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">8</span> <div><strong>Why does Descartes need to prove God's existence before he can establish any further certain knowledge?</strong></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8a" id="q8a">
        <label for="q8a">Without God, Descartes cannot explain where the physical world came from.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8b" id="q8b">
        <label for="q8b">God's existence is required by religious orthodoxy, and Descartes wrote for a theological audience.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8c" id="q8c">
        <label for="q8c">Only a non-deceiving God can guarantee that clear and distinct perception tracks the truth — without this guarantee, every conclusion drawn by reason might be systematically false.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8d" id="q8d">
        <label for="q8d">God must exist to explain why the cogito is self-evident rather than just another possibly false belief.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. The Evil Demon hypothesis is still undefeated after the cogito. Descartes needs a non-deceiving God to rule out the possibility that his reasoning faculty is systematically corrupted — only then can clear and distinct perception be trusted.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Without God, the Evil Demon hypothesis remains in play. Descartes needs to prove that a non-deceiving God created his rational faculties — only then can he trust that clear and distinct perceptions are reliable rather than the product of systematic deception.</div>
    <button class="quiz__clear" type="button" style="display:none;">Clear</button>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q9b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">9</span> <div><strong>In Meditation IV, Descartes explains how error is possible even though God is not a deceiver. What is his explanation?</strong></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9a" id="q9a">
        <label for="q9a">God gave humans a limited intellect and an equally limited will, so errors arise from the smallness of both.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9b" id="q9b">
        <label for="q9b">Error arises when the unlimited will extends its judgments beyond what the finite intellect perceives clearly and distinctly.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9c" id="q9c">
        <label for="q9c">The body interferes with the mind's reasoning, introducing sensory distortions that produce false beliefs.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9d" id="q9d">
        <label for="q9d">Error is unavoidable given human finitude — God permits it as part of a greater plan we cannot understand.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Descartes distinguishes between the intellect (finite — it does not perceive everything clearly) and the will (unlimited — it can affirm or deny anything). Error occurs when the will judges matters the intellect has not yet perceived clearly and distinctly.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">In Meditation IV, Descartes argues that the intellect is limited but the will is unlimited. Error arises when we use our unlimited will to judge things our finite intellect has not yet perceived clearly and distinctly. The fault lies in misusing our freedom, not in God's design.</div>
    <button class="quiz__clear" type="button" style="display:none;">Clear</button>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q10c">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">10</span> <div><strong>In Meditation V, Descartes offers an ontological argument for God's existence. What is the core claim of this argument?</strong></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10a" id="q10a">
        <label for="q10a">God must exist because someone had to create the physical world, and only an infinite being could do so.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10b" id="q10b">
        <label for="q10b">The widespread human belief in God across cultures proves that God exists as the common cause of that belief.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10c" id="q10c">
        <label for="q10c">Existence is itself a perfection; since God is defined as the most perfect being, God must possess existence — a God lacking existence would not be the most perfect being.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10d" id="q10d">
        <label for="q10d">God's existence is confirmed by the reliability of mathematical truths, which could only be guaranteed by a perfect mind.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. The ontological argument holds that existence is a perfection — part of what it means to be maximally perfect. Just as a triangle necessarily has interior angles summing to 180 degrees, God necessarily exists because existence is contained in the very concept of a most perfect being.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">The ontological argument argues from the concept of God alone. Since God is defined as the most perfect being, and existence is a perfection, God must exist — a non-existent God would lack a perfection and therefore not be the most perfect being conceivable.</div>
    <button class="quiz__clear" type="button" style="display:none;">Clear</button>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q11b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">11</span> <div><strong>In Meditation VI, Descartes argues for a "real distinction" between mind and body. What does "real distinction" mean in this context?</strong></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q11" value="q11a" id="q11a">
        <label for="q11a">Mind and body are two different ways of describing the same underlying substance.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q11" value="q11b" id="q11b">
        <label for="q11b">Mind and body are genuinely distinct substances — each could in principle exist without the other.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q11" value="q11c" id="q11c">
        <label for="q11c">The mind is more real than the body because it is better known.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q11" value="q11d" id="q11d">
        <label for="q11d">Mind and body are distinct only in the sense that they have different functions within a single unified human being.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. A &quot;real distinction&quot; means the two things are genuinely separate substances — not just different aspects of one thing. Descartes argues he can clearly and distinctly conceive of mind without body and body without mind, so God could create either one without the other.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">A &quot;real distinction&quot; for Descartes means the two things are genuinely distinct substances that could exist independently. He argues that since he can clearly conceive of mind without body and vice versa, they must be separate kinds of things — not just different names for the same thing.</div>
    <button class="quiz__clear" type="button" style="display:none;">Clear</button>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q12c">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">12</span> <div><strong>How does Descartes argue in Meditation VI that the external material world exists?</strong></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q12" value="q12a" id="q12a">
        <label for="q12a">He uses the cogito to infer that if he exists, the world he perceives must also exist.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q12" value="q12b" id="q12b">
        <label for="q12b">He argues that God directly reveals the existence of the external world through religious experience.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q12" value="q12c" id="q12c">
        <label for="q12c">He has an involuntary, irresistible tendency to believe in the external world; a non-deceiving God would not have given him such a tendency if the world did not exist.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q12" value="q12d" id="q12d">
        <label for="q12d">He reasons that the coherence and consistency of his sensory experiences over time proves that an external world causes them.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Descartes argues that he has a powerful, involuntary inclination to believe in the external world — one he did not choose. Since God is not a deceiver, God would not have created him with an irresistible tendency toward a false belief. Therefore the external world exists.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Descartes argues that his involuntary tendency to believe in the external world — which he cannot help having — must have been placed in him by God. Since God does not deceive, this irresistible inclination must be pointing toward something real.</div>
    <button class="quiz__clear" type="button" style="display:none;">Clear</button>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q13b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">13</span> <div><strong>What is the "Cartesian Circle," and why is it considered a serious objection to Descartes' project?</strong></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q13" value="q13a" id="q13a">
        <label for="q13a">Descartes argues in a circle by using the body to explain the mind and then using the mind to explain the body.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q13" value="q13b" id="q13b">
        <label for="q13b">Descartes uses clear and distinct perception to prove God exists, then uses God to validate clear and distinct perception — each step depends on the other.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q13" value="q13c" id="q13c">
        <label for="q13c">Descartes uses the cogito to begin his argument but then abandons it, returning to it only at the end — a circular structure.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q13" value="q13d" id="q13d">
        <label for="q13d">Descartes proves the external world exists using God, but uses the external world to explain where our idea of God comes from.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. The Cartesian Circle is the charge that Descartes uses clear and distinct perception to prove God exists, then uses God's non-deceptive nature to validate clear and distinct perception. If one step is in doubt, both collapse — making the argument circular.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">The Cartesian Circle is the charge of circular reasoning: Descartes needs clear and distinct perception to prove God, and needs God to validate clear and distinct perception. Each depends on the other, which means neither provides independent support for the other.</div>
    <button class="quiz__clear" type="button" style="display:none;">Clear</button>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q14c">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">14</span> <div><strong>The Meditations is often described as a "foundationalist" project. What does this mean?</strong></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q14" value="q14a" id="q14a">
        <label for="q14a">Descartes argues that all knowledge ultimately rests on religious faith as its foundation.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q14" value="q14b" id="q14b">
        <label for="q14b">Descartes believes the foundations of knowledge were established by ancient Greek philosophers and need only to be recovered.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q14" value="q14c" id="q14c">
        <label for="q14c">Descartes seeks to identify a set of absolutely certain basic beliefs from which all other knowledge can be derived by deduction.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q14" value="q14d" id="q14d">
        <label for="q14d">Descartes argues that scientific observation provides the only reliable foundation for knowledge claims.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Foundationalism holds that knowledge rests on a set of basic beliefs that are themselves certain without requiring further justification. For Descartes, the cogito is the foundational belief, and everything else — God's existence, the reliability of reason, the external world — is built from it deductively.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Foundationalism is the view that knowledge rests on certain basic beliefs that need no further justification. Descartes' project is foundationalist because he seeks one indubitable starting point — the cogito — and builds all other knowledge from it through deductive reasoning.</div>
    <button class="quiz__clear" type="button" style="display:none;">Clear</button>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q15b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">15</span> <div><strong>Descartes' substance dualism — the view that mind and body are genuinely distinct substances — immediately generates what serious philosophical problem?</strong></div></div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q15" value="q15a" id="q15a">
        <label for="q15a">If mind and body are distinct, it is unclear why God would have created both instead of just the mind.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q15" value="q15b" id="q15b">
        <label for="q15b">If mind is unextended and body is extended, it is unclear how they can causally interact — how a thought can move a limb, or physical damage produce mental pain.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q15" value="q15c" id="q15c">
        <label for="q15c">If the mind is non-physical, it cannot be studied scientifically, making psychology impossible.</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q15" value="q15d" id="q15d">
        <label for="q15d">If the body is merely mechanical, it implies that animals have no minds and therefore no moral status.</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. This is the interaction problem. If mind has no spatial extension and body is defined entirely by extension and motion, there is no obvious mechanism by which one could affect the other. Descartes acknowledged the problem but never solved it satisfactorily.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">The most immediate problem with substance dualism is the interaction problem: if the mind is entirely non-physical and the body is entirely physical, how can they causally affect each other? How does a decision (mental) cause a hand to move (physical)? Descartes could not fully answer this.</div>
    <button class="quiz__clear" type="button" style="display:none;">Clear</button>
  </div>
  

